WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.579
A coffee-shop app sends every card payment to Kafka, a program that keeps logs of records and lets other programs read them.

2
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:16.456
The payments live in a log called payments-0, stored as files on one computer's disk.

3
00:00:16.456 --> 00:00:19.349
The program running Kafka there is a broker.

4
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:20.859
Now that disk dies.

5
00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:23.570
Nobody can write to payments-0 or read it.

6
00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.055
And if the disk is gone for good, so are the payments.

7
00:00:27.055 --> 00:00:30.604
How do we keep them, and when is a payment really safe?

8
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:34.750
Picture a small shop that keeps three copies of its sales book.

9
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:41.653
The owner writes each sale in her book, and two assistants copy it into theirs, line by line, in the same order.

10
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:46.406
The house rule: a sale counts once everyone in the room has copied it.

11
00:00:46.406 --> 00:00:47.887
Keep that rule in mind.

12
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:50.744
Kafka does the same, once per partition.

13
00:00:50.744 --> 00:00:54.224
Each copy is a replica, and three is the usual number.

14
00:00:54.224 --> 00:00:59.444
The owner's copy is the leader: every write goes to it, and it decides the order.

15
00:00:59.444 --> 00:01:01.313
The assistants are followers.

16
00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:07.052
Only the leader gives out offsets, so offset 4 means the same payment on every broker.

17
00:01:07.052 --> 00:01:11.453
Each follower keeps asking the leader: anything after my last record?

18
00:01:11.453 --> 00:01:14.515
It appends what comes back, at the same offsets.

19
00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:20.016
Each replica has its own end offset: the offset its next record will get.

20
00:01:20.016 --> 00:01:23.612
Here, every copy holds 0 to 4, so each one's is 5.

21
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:27.934
Now a producer, the program that writes, sends three payments.

22
00:01:27.934 --> 00:01:30.230
The leader adds them at 5, 6 and 7.

23
00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:31.542
Its end offset is 8.

24
00:01:31.542 --> 00:01:34.560
When should it tell the producer they're safe?

25
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:40.462
Answer right away, and the producer may hear safe a moment before the leader's disk dies.

26
00:01:40.462 --> 00:01:41.806
Those three are lost.

27
00:01:41.806 --> 00:01:46.157
Wait for every follower, and one slow follower holds up every write.

28
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.064
Kafka takes a middle path.

29
00:01:48.064 --> 00:01:54.207
The leader keeps a list of the replicas keeping up with it: the in-sync replicas, ISR for short.

30
00:01:54.207 --> 00:01:56.127
That's the people in the room.

31
00:01:56.367 --> 00:02:01.183
From them it works out the high watermark: the smallest end offset in the ISR.

32
00:02:01.183 --> 00:02:04.764
It's the line up to which everyone in the room has copied.

33
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.787
Records below it are on every in-sync copy.

34
00:02:07.787 --> 00:02:09.408
They're called committed.

35
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.752
Broker 2 fetches 5 to 7.

36
00:02:11.752 --> 00:02:13.461
Its end offset is 8.

37
00:02:13.461 --> 00:02:14.915
Broker 3 is slow.

38
00:02:14.915 --> 00:02:16.283
It's still at 5.

39
00:02:16.283 --> 00:02:18.676
Where is the high watermark?

40
00:02:18.676 --> 00:02:20.044
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:23.416
5.

42
00:02:23.416 --> 00:02:27.158
The smallest end offset in the room is broker 3's.

43
00:02:27.158 --> 00:02:32.695
So consumers still can't see 5, 6 or 7, and the producer is still waiting.

44
00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:35.054
Then broker 3 catches up to 8.

45
00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:39.508
The high watermark jumps to 8, and the producer gets its answer.

46
00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:44.154
The producer picks how long to wait with a setting called acks.

47
00:02:44.154 --> 00:02:47.009
acks=1: answer once your own copy has it.

48
00:02:47.009 --> 00:02:53.973
acks=all: answer once every in-sync replica has it, the moment the high watermark passes the record.

49
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.229
acks=all has been the default since Kafka 3.0.

50
00:02:57.229 --> 00:03:03.813
And because consumers read only below the high watermark, they never see a record that could vanish.

51
00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:10.165
When the leader's broker fails, a follower takes over, but only one that has every committed record.

52
00:03:10.433 --> 00:03:17.424
Electing one that missed some would make them disappear without any error, because its shorter log becomes the truth.

53
00:03:17.424 --> 00:03:21.427
That's an unclean leader election, and Kafka forbids it by default.

54
00:03:21.427 --> 00:03:24.832
The partition stops taking writes instead of losing data.

55
00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:27.051
And one gap in the house rule.

56
00:03:27.051 --> 00:03:30.755
If both assistants go home, the room is the owner alone.

57
00:03:30.755 --> 00:03:33.599
Everyone in the room has copied every sale.

58
00:03:33.599 --> 00:03:35.120
It's a very small room.

59
00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:42.425
So: one leader decides the order, followers copy it, and a record counts once everyone in the room has it.

60
00:03:42.425 --> 00:03:46.153
The room is the ISR, and the line is the high watermark.

61
00:03:46.633 --> 00:03:47.805
One quick check.

62
00:03:47.805 --> 00:03:53.590
The leader's end offset is 9, broker 2 is at 7, and broker 3 at 9, all in sync.

63
00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.691
Where's the high watermark, and what can consumers read?

64
00:03:57.691 --> 00:03:58.863
Pause and think.

65
00:04:02.067 --> 00:04:02.244
7.

66
00:04:02.244 --> 00:04:05.256
Consumers can read up to offset 6.

67
00:04:05.256 --> 00:04:07.825
Broker 2 holds everyone back.
